==============================================================================
STAGE 0d -- per-operation or per-element? batch scaling of the Jacobian cost
==============================================================================
  float64, CPU, torch 2.5.1, threads 6
  production batch is 256 (config.py batch_size)

   batch    step [s]   jvp 1 dir    ratio   jacfwd 10    ratio     fwd+bwd     jvp f+b    ratio
      64    0.003540    0.046480   13.13x    0.077600   21.92x    0.014563    0.059433    4.08x
     256    0.004873    0.044004    9.03x    0.074184   15.22x    0.017751    0.056622    3.19x
     512    0.004695    0.045751    9.74x    0.083380   17.76x    0.018488    0.059729    3.23x
    2048    0.007919    0.069582    8.79x    0.131581   16.62x    0.032456    0.102132    3.15x

  --- reading ---
  jvp/step ratio: 13.13x at batch 64 -> 8.79x at batch 2048
  the ratio FALLS with batch: the cost is per-OPERATION (transform wrapper and the per-call M(Y) rebuild), not per-element. The production ratio is the one at the production batch, and an analytic implementation would attack exactly this overhead.

  AT THE PRODUCTION BATCH 256, which is the number the design is judged on:
    forward only:        one jvp is 9.03x a plain step
    forward + backward:  3.19x, i.e. a training step costs that much more with the subtraction than without
    one nf = 400 window: 7.10 s -> 22.65 s
